const COURSE_17_QUIZZES_DEF = {
  c17q_l1:{ title:'Quiz: Method instead of guessing', qs:[
    { q:'A user reports “the internet is down”. What do you do first?',
      opts:['I reboot the router and the switch, since that is usually the quickest path to an immediate fix','I find out who and what exactly fails, since when it lasts and what changed in the network before','I replace the user’s network cable and port, because cables tend to be the most common cause'],
      correct:1, why:'A complaint must first become a fault description: who, what exactly, since when and what changed. Without it you guess – and a reboot even erases the evidence.' },
    { q:'What is the fault’s scope good for when troubleshooting?',
      opts:['it says what the repair will cost and whether buying a new device beats fixing the old','it narrows the suspects: one user points down to them, everyone points up to the shared path','it determines which vendor is liable, and thereby decides who the fault gets reported to'],
      correct:1, why:'Scope is the cheapest tool you have. One affected person points at their cable, port and settings; everyone affected points at the gateway, firewall, uplink or DNS.' },
    { q:'What does “halving the problem” mean and why is it worth it?',
      opts:['you test a point in the middle of the path and one test rules out a whole half of the suspects','you split the repair between two technicians so each does half the work and it goes twice as fast','you cut the traffic in half so the fault shows more clearly and becomes easier to catch'],
      correct:0, why:'Each test in the middle discards half the suspects. If a packet reaches the gateway, the lower half is healthy; if not, the upper half is irrelevant.' },
    { q:'Why should you change only one thing at a time when fixing?',
      opts:['so the intervention lasts as long as possible and there is more time to write detailed documentation','otherwise you know neither which change helped nor whether one of them broke something else','because devices cannot accept more than one configuration change within the same hour'],
      correct:1, why:'Three changes at once mean three unknowns. With one change and immediate verification you always know what it did – and can revert it if it did not help.' }
  ]},
  c17q_l2:{ title:'Quiz: The physical layer', qs:[
    { q:'A port reports the connection is down (link down). What is worth pursuing?',
      opts:['the port’s VLAN assignment and routes on the gateway, since they decide whether the link passes','the cable and its ends, or whether an admin disabled the port; higher settings will not fix it','the computer’s address and mask, because without a correct mask no link can come up at all'],
      correct:1, why:'While the physical layer is down, VLANs, addresses and routes are pointless – no setting revives a dead cable. Look at the cable, its ends and a disabled port.' },
    { q:'How does “admin down” differ from a broken cable?',
      opts:['in nothing essential, both mean faulty hardware that needs replacing as soon as possible','admin down appears only on fiber, whereas copper cabling always reports as a broken link','the hardware and cable are fine; the port is off by an admin’s decision, not by a fault'],
      correct:2, why:'Admin down is a decision, not a fault. Unused ports are routinely disabled for security – so before enabling one, find out why it was disabled.' },
    { q:'Users report “slow internet” and stuttering calls. What does that suggest?',
      opts:['a connection somewhere on the path is faulty or congested and loses frames without going down','someone changed names to addresses in the network, so resolution now runs slower than it used to','one of the computers has a wrong mask and needlessly sends traffic around via the default gateway'],
      correct:0, why:'Partial frame loss does not look like an outage but like a slow, unreliable network. Do distinguish a faulty connection from a merely congested line, though.' },
    { q:'What does “line” mean in this application, unlike a “connection”?',
      opts:['a line is the physical cable in the socket, whereas a connection is its label in the patch panel','a line is the connection’s parameters – speed and a possible outage; the connection is the link on the canvas','a line runs between two buildings, whereas a connection is used only inside a single room'],
      correct:1, why:'A cable is the physical thing, a connection is the edge between devices on the canvas, and the line is its parameters. A connection can be up while the line is misconfigured or congested.' }
  ]},
  c17q_l3:{ title:'Quiz: VLANs and ports', qs:[
    { q:'How do you recognize an access port assigned to the wrong VLAN?',
      opts:['the port stops reporting and shows on the switch as permanently disabled, though the cable is fine','the computer has an address from a different range than colleagues and sees devices it should not','every computer in the building loses connectivity at once, because switching in the switch stops'],
      correct:1, why:'From outside everything looks healthy: the cable holds and the port is up. The giveaway is an address from a foreign range and visibility of foreign devices – the device sits in another network.' },
    { q:'Where does the fix belong when a port puts a computer in a foreign VLAN?',
      opts:['on the switch port, where the VLAN assignment gets fixed; rewriting the address only masks it','in the computer’s settings, where the address and mask get retyped to match the target network','on the default gateway, where a route between both VLANs is added so traffic finds a way anyway'],
      correct:0, why:'The device physically sits in the wrong network. Rewriting the address is a patch after which the machine keeps misbehaving – the port assignment on the switch is the correct fix.' },
    { q:'Inside a switch all works, between switches only for some groups. What do you look for?',
      opts:['the default gateway on the computers, because without it frames never get between switches','the trunk and its allowed-VLAN list, or a configuration mismatch at the two ends of the link','flushing the name cache on both switches so they memorize the correct paths to the VLANs'],
      correct:1, why:'That symptom is typical of a trunk: the connection is up and other VLANs cross it, but yours is not allowed on it. Always check both ends, not just one.' },
    { q:'Two devices in the same VLAN cannot see each other. What now?',
      opts:['add a route on the gateway, because without one no frame can be delivered in layer two at all','configure a different resolver on both, because otherwise they cannot find each other by name','stay in layer two: verify both port assignments and the connection; leave gateway and routes alone'],
      correct:2, why:'Devices in one VLAN must see each other without a router. Until they do, the gateway and routes are irrelevant – and a common mistake is checking only one of the two ports.' }
  ]},
  c17q_l4:{ title:'Quiz: Addresses, gateway, paths', qs:[
    { q:'A computer sees everything on its network but nothing outside. What is most likely?',
      opts:['a missing or wrong default gateway, or one whose address lies outside its own network','a faulty cable between the computer and the switch that passes frames in one direction only','a congested resolver that stopped answering name lookups because of the query volume'],
      correct:0, why:'This pair of symptoms is typical of the gateway: inside the network delivery is direct, outward it is handed to the gateway. When that is missing or unreachable, nothing gets out.' },
    { q:'What does a too-wide subnet mask on a computer cause?',
      opts:['the computer loses its address entirely and must assign itself a fallback that gets it nowhere','the computer treats foreign networks as neighbors, addresses them directly and gets no reply back','the computer starts sending all traffic to the resolver, because it cannot tell where to look for a target'],
      correct:1, why:'The mask decides “neighbor or via the gateway”. Too wide sends traffic directly where direct fails; too narrow pushes neighbors needlessly through the gateway.' },
    { q:'Why does “I get no reply” not mean the request never arrived?',
      opts:['because replies always have lower priority and get dropped before requests when the network loads','because the far side may not know the route back to your network, so the reply never returns','because a firewall always drops replies until an admin manually permits return traffic'],
      correct:1, why:'Traffic is bidirectional and either half can be missing. If the far side has no return path, your request arrives but the reply does not – and the target looks dead.' },
    { q:'How do you find where the path breaks when the topology is longer?',
      opts:['you watch how far traffic gets; the fault lies between the last device that replies and the first silent one','you swap every device on the path for a new one in turn, until the connection starts working','you reboot all routers on the path at once, because that rebuilds the routing tables from scratch'],
      correct:0, why:'It is the halving from lesson one applied to the path. The last replying device is the marker; between it and the first silent one, check the route out and back.' }
  ]},
  c17q_l5:{ title:'Quiz: Names and DNS', qs:[
    { q:'The server does not open by name but does by address. Where do you look?',
      opts:['at cables and ports, because a name crosses the network by a different path than a numeric address','at name resolution: the network and paths are healthy, the resolver or the record itself fails','at the mask and gateway, because names resolve beyond the gateway while addresses go directly'],
      correct:1, why:'Connecting by address proved the path and rules work. Only one broken part remains: turning the name into an address. Stop examining the network.' },
    { q:'External sites work, internal company names do not. What is the typical cause?',
      opts:['the computer is configured with a resolver that has no answers for the company’s internal names','the default gateway drops internal traffic, because it treats it as an attempt to bypass rules','internal names are always longer, so they no longer fit whole into the resolver’s answer'],
      correct:0, why:'Only the local resolver knows internal names. With a public one configured, public sites work and company names do not – the difference is whom it asks.' },
    { q:'You fixed the record, but it works for some and not others. Why?',
      opts:['the record got fixed on only one resolver and the other has not yet synchronized with it at all','devices hold the old answer in cache and it holds until its TTL expires; then things settle down','a firewall rule lets only some users through and drops the others’ traffic to the new server'],
      correct:1, why:'Every device’s cache is of a different age, hence the apparent illogic. You spot it because behavior differs by device and the problem fades on its own.' },
    { q:'What do you do in advance before moving a service to a different address?',
      opts:['you lower the record’s TTL so the change spreads fast and nobody goes to the old address','you raise the record’s TTL so devices keep the answer stored as long as possible and ask less','you switch the resolver off for the move, so no incomplete answer gets stored during it'],
      correct:0, why:'While the old TTL runs, the old answer holds. Lowering the TTL in advance shortens the window in which users would go to an address where nobody lives.' }
  ]},
  c17q_l6:{ title:'Quiz: When a rule is to blame', qs:[
    { q:'Everything else works, only one service is silent. What does that suggest?',
      opts:['a broken cable to that server, since it passes only the traffic of the other applications','a firewall rule: the default deny drops traffic that nobody has explicitly permitted','an exhausted routing table on the gateway, which had no room left for another path'],
      correct:1, why:'Selectivity is a rule’s signature: a network fault usually kills everything to the target, whereas a rule hits only a certain kind of traffic. A new service without a rule is the classic.' },
    { q:'The rule permitting access is in the list – and it still fails. Why?',
      opts:['a broader rule higher in the list matches the same traffic and decides before yours does','rules are applied from the bottom up, so the very last one in the list always decides','the rule activates only after a firewall reboot; until then it merely appears in the list'],
      correct:0, why:'A firewall uses the first matching rule from the top. A broader rule above shadows yours and makes it dead – hence specific rules belong on top and general ones below.' },
    { q:'Why is shadowing by a broad permit worse than shadowing by a deny?',
      opts:['because a permit loads the firewall far more than a deny and slows down all the other traffic','because nothing works at all and finding the cause then takes far longer than with a plain deny','because it shows no symptom: you believe you are blocking, yet traffic quietly passes all along'],
      correct:2, why:'A shadowed deny is a quiet security hole – nobody complains, because everything “works”. Hence after every change you also verify what must not work.' },
    { q:'You went through every layer and still do not know. What is the right next step?',
      opts:['isolate by swapping one element, note the verified steps and escalate in time when options run out','try various configuration changes in quick succession until the connection happens to come up','reset the whole topology to defaults and rebuild it from scratch according to the documentation'],
      correct:0, why:'Random changes only muddy the evidence. Isolation halves the suspects, documentation spares whoever takes over, and escalation is the right decision, not a defeat.' }
  ]}
};
